Vesicles are objects consisting essentially of a closed membrane. Soap bubbles are a good example. In biology the membrane is invariably a phospholipid bilayer. Much ought to be said about the interesting physics of such systems, but here we will restrict to the set of problems which arises when the membrane is described as a twodimensional fluid1 elastic. This means, there exists (i) a surface tension and (ii) a bending energy involved with “out-of-the-plane” motions of the membrane. Following Helfrich [11], the (free) energy per unit area of membrane is then given by
